<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{.Title}}</title>
{{- /* THE VIEW'S WORDS, like the <title> and the chart's <desc>. This was the
       literal written for the single page this template used to render, so
       spending.html shipped a description of revenue and revenue.html one of
       divisions -- one line above the two elements that had just been made
       per-view. */}}
<meta name="description" content="{{.ChartDescription}}">
<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
<script>
  // Stamp the saved theme before first paint. Inline and tiny on purpose: a
  // deferred script would repaint the page in the wrong mode first.
  try {
    var t = localStorage.getItem("fisc-theme");
    if (t === "light" || t === "dark") document.documentElement.dataset.theme = t;
  } catch (e) { /* private mode: fall back to the OS setting */ }
</script>
</head>
<body>

<div class="wip-banner" role="status">⚠️ Work in progress — early preview, coverage is incomplete.</div>

{{- if gt (len .Nav) 1}}
<nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Views">
  <ul>
    {{- range .Nav}}
    <li>{{if .Current}}<span aria-current="page">{{.Label}}</span>{{else}}<a href="{{.Path}}">{{.Label}}</a>{{end}}</li>
    {{- end}}
  </ul>
</nav>
{{- end}}

<header class="page-head">
  <div>
    <h1>{{.Title}}</h1>
    <p class="lede">{{.Lede}}</p>
  </div>
  <div class="head-controls">
    {{- if gt (len .Years) 1}}
    <fieldset class="year-toggle" id="year-toggle" disabled>
      <legend>Budget year</legend>
      {{- range $i, $y := .Years}}
      <input type="radio" name="fisc-year" id="year-{{$y.Stem}}" value="{{$y.Stem}}"
             {{if eq $i 0}}checked{{end}}>
      <label for="year-{{$y.Stem}}">{{$y.Label}}</label>
      {{- end}}
    </fieldset>
    {{- end}}
    <button type="button" id="theme-toggle" aria-pressed="false">Dark mode</button>
  </div>
</header>

<main>

<!-- THIS PAGE RENDERS NO STAT TILES AND NO HEADLINE, and that is its central
     design constraint rather than a gap (fisc-5miz.6).

     The document behind it holds the same money at more than one grain: 44
     facts sit behind both a division's object rows and the fund-to-division
     link that totals them. So a "total spending" tile, a column total, or a
     table a reader could add up would be WRONG QUIETLY -- every figure on it
     tying to a fact, every check green. internal/project publishes no headline
     for this document for the same reason, and
     docs/general-fund-drilldown-contract.md's "No headline" section is the
     argument. There is no <section class="figures"> here and buildDrilldownPage
     hands the client no Hero and no Figures, so a year switch cannot put one
     back either. -->

<h2>The flow</h2>
{{- /* THE PAGE-SPECIFIC WORDS ARE {{.Lede}} IN THE HEADER, and this block is
       what is true of BOTH pages this template renders. Three paragraphs here
       used to describe a three-column chart -- revenue, fund group, division --
       which is the shape neither Revenue nor Spending draws. Prose that
       describes one page's columns cannot live in a template two pages share;
       it goes in the view, which is the only place that knows. */}}
{{- /* THE INSTRUCTION IS REPAINTED, because it stops being true the moment a
       reader follows it. In an opened view nothing is drillable -- the drill is
       one hop, so a node in the opened chart is not itself openable -- and the
       sentence would go on inviting a click that does nothing. app.js's
       paintChartHint owns the span; this is the opening state, server-rendered
       so it survives with JavaScript off, where it is also true because nothing
       can be opened at all. */}}
<p class="lede">
  <span id="lede-year">{{.FiscalYearLabel}} {{.Basis}}</span>.
  {{- if .Drill}} <span id="chart-hint">Click a node in the right-hand column to open it into
  its parts, or tab to one and press Enter.</span>{{end}}
</p>
<div class="card">
  <div class="controls">
    <div class="legend" id="legend" aria-label="Fund groups"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="legend" aria-label="How to read the marks">
    <span><span class="key published" aria-hidden="true"></span> printed by the city</span>
    <span><span class="key derived" aria-hidden="true"></span> ◇ our inference (dashed)</span>
    {{- /* WHAT A CLICK DOES IS PER VIEW, so what this sentence promises has to
           be too. On a page that drills, activating a node OPENS it and does
           not isolate -- see site/app.js's node handlers and export.Drill --
           so the old wording would have been an instruction that does nothing
           on either of the site's two drilling pages. */}}
    <span class="subtle">Hover, tap or tab to a flow for its amount, fact ids and sources.
      {{- /* NO PROMISE ABOUT THE SWATCHES ON A PAGE THAT DRILLS. This said the
             fund swatches still follow one group's money, and on spending.html
             the legend is empty -- buildLegend draws a button per fund group
             that the chart TOUCHES, and that page's two columns are one fund
             and its divisions. Measured: 6 buttons on revenue.html's overview,
             0 on spending.html and 0 on all 29 opened views. */}}
      {{- /* THE SWATCH SENTENCE IS BACK, CONDITIONALLY. It was dropped for every
             drilling page on a measurement -- "0 legend buttons" -- that holds
             for spending.html and for every OPENED view, and not for
             revenue.html's overview, which draws six clickable fund-group
             swatches nothing then explained. buildLegend draws one per fund
             group the chart touches, which is a property of the tier set, so
             the condition is the tier set. */}}
      {{- /* THE SWATCH SENTENCE MOVED INTO #chart-hint TOO. It was conditional
             on the OPENING state's legend and never repainted, so it went on
             offering swatches in every opened view, where buildLegend draws
             none. What is left here is true whatever the chart is showing. */}}
      {{if .Drill}}Escape closes an opened node and clears a selection.{{else}}Click a
      node or a fund swatch to follow only that money; Escape puts the rest back.{{end}}</span>
  </div>
  {{- if .Drill}}
  {{- /* SHIPPED EMPTY AND hidden, filled by app.js. A breadcrumb rendered
         server-side would name a node nobody has opened yet, and with
         JavaScript off there is no drill to come back from -- the chart draws
         its overview and never changes. Same reasoning as the year toggle
         shipping disabled: a control that cannot do anything must not look as
         though it can. */}}
  <nav class="breadcrumb" id="breadcrumb" aria-label="Chart depth" hidden></nav>
  {{- end}}
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <svg class="sankey" id="chart" role="group" aria-labelledby="chart-title chart-desc">
      {{- /* THE PACKAGER'S ONE STRING, not a composition of this template's
             own. app.js repaints this element from the same yearView field on
             a year switch and appends to it on a drill, so a second wording
             composed here is an accessible name that silently reverts on the
             first toggle -- announced only to the readers who cannot see the
             marks and check. The <desc> stays the view's words, served whole. */}}
      <title id="chart-title">{{.ChartTitle}}</title>
      <desc id="chart-desc">
        {{.ChartDescription}} The same figures are in the flow table below, which opens
        from the "Every flow the chart draws, as a table" heading.
      </desc>
    </svg>
    <div class="tooltip" id="tooltip" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></div>
  </div>
  <div class="detail" id="detail" aria-live="polite">
    <p class="subtle">Select a flow or a node to pin its provenance here.</p>
  </div>
  <noscript>
    <p>
      This page draws its chart in the browser from
      <a href="{{.DataPath}}">{{.DataPath}}</a>. With JavaScript off, that file is the whole
      story: every amount, fact id and source page is in it, at a finer grain than the chart
      draws.
    </p>
  </noscript>
</div>

{{- /* THIS USED TO WARN THAT ADDING THE FLOWS UP DOUBLE-COUNTS, which was
       true of the three-column page and is false of both pages that replaced
       it: {0,2} and {3,4} are one grain each, and their ribbons sum. The
       document still holds two grains -- that is why it publishes no headline
       -- but a reader looking at either of these charts cannot reach the
       second one from it. Warning about a trap the page does not have teaches
       a reader to distrust a figure that is fine. */}}
<p class="lede">
  <strong>This page publishes no total.</strong> The document behind it holds the same money
  at more than one grain — fund totals and the object rows beneath them — so no single number
  covers it without counting part of it twice. Each chart here draws one pair of columns,
  which do sum; the totals the city itself publishes are on
  <a href="index.html">the budget flows page</a>.
</p>

{{- /* THE APPARATUS IS DISCLOSED HERE TOO, on index.html.tmpl's pattern and for
       its reasons, which that file's comment sets out. The <h2>s stay inside
       the <summary>, so the outline a screen reader walks is unchanged.

       AND THE CHART'S <desc> HAD TO MOVE WITH THEM. It said the same figures
       were "listed in the table below", which stops being true the moment the
       table ships closed: a collapsed <details> is out of the accessibility
       tree and out of in-page find, so the sentence was a promise to exactly
       the readers who cannot look and check. */}}
<details class="apparatus" id="derived-view">
  <summary>
    <h2>What we inferred</h2>
    <span class="apparatus-hint">the classifications that are ours, not the city's</span>
  </summary>
  <p class="lede">
    A figure the city printed and a classification we made are different things, so they are
    never drawn alike. Everything listed here is ours, with the reasoning and the row it came
    from.
  </p>
  <ul class="derived-list" id="derived-list">
    <li class="subtle">Loading from {{.DataPath}}…</li>
  </ul>
</details>

{{- /* NO #caveats-count IN THIS SUMMARY, unlike index.html.tmpl's. That id is
       repainted per year and this template renders no year control, so a count
       here would be server-rendered once and never corrected. app.js reaches it
       with maybeEl precisely so that this page may omit it. */}}
<details class="apparatus" id="caveats-view">
  <summary>
    <h2>Caveats</h2>
    <span class="apparatus-hint">why this chart will not reconcile row-for-row against
      the printed schedules</span>
  </summary>
  <p class="lede">
    These are the reasons this chart will not reconcile row-for-row against the printed
    schedules. They ship with the data, not in a footnote.
  </p>
  <ul class="caveats" id="caveats">
    {{- range .Caveats}}
    <li>{{if .Href}}<a href="{{.Href}}">{{.Summary}}</a>{{else}}{{.Summary}}{{end}}</li>
    {{- end}}
  </ul>
</details>

<!-- THE TABLE IS THE CHART'S TWIN AND NOT THE FILE'S. app.js builds it from the
     FOLDED document, so it holds one row per ribbon.

     WHAT THE FOLD DOES WITH THE REST IS NOT "their fact ids ride on the rows
     that absorbed them", which is what this said. That held at the
     three-column tier set, where a link folding into another carried its facts
     along. At both tier sets shipped here the OTHER side of the document folds
     to self-loops, which foldDocument drops -- so those facts are cited on the
     other page rather than on a row here, 190 and 49 of the document's 239.
     The paragraph below says so; this comment was still saying the opposite.

     The count is server-rendered from the FILE and repainted to what was drawn,
     so a reader with JavaScript off is told what the file holds and a reader
     with a chart in front of them is told what the chart shows -- and since
     that repaint, both numbers, so the gap between them is stated rather than
     inferred. -->
{{- /* #counts-line MOVED INTO THE SUMMARY and its words did not change.
       paintCounts reaches it by id and tools/jscheck/year.mjs matches it by
       regex in this file AND in index.html.tmpl, then asserts the two wordings
       agree -- none of which cares where the span sits. A reader deciding
       whether to open the table is who the sentence was written for. */}}
<details class="apparatus" id="table-view">
  <summary>
    <h2>Every flow the chart draws, as a table</h2>
    <span class="apparatus-hint">the chart's twin, same numbers —
      <span id="counts-line">{{.Links}} flows between {{.Nodes}} nodes, from {{.Facts}} facts</span></span>
  </summary>
  <p class="lede">
  {{- /* THE OLD SENTENCE PROMISED that every fact behind a merged flow is
         listed on the row that carries it, "so nothing here is cited less
         precisely than the file cites it". True at the three-column tier set,
         where the surviving fund-to-division link carries the object rows'
         facts. False at both shipped ones: each folds the OTHER side into
         self-loops and drops them, so this page's rows cite 190 of the
         document's 239 facts and the other page's the remaining 49. Nothing is
         lost -- the two pages partition it -- but this page cannot claim to
         carry it all. */}}
    Every fact behind a flow drawn here is listed on its row. This page draws one side of the
    document, so the facts behind the other side are cited on the page that draws it, and both
    are in <a href="{{.DataPath}}">{{.DataPath}}</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table id="flow-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">From</th>
          <th scope="col">To</th>
          <th scope="col">Amount</th>
          <th scope="col">Kind</th>
          <th scope="col">Basis</th>
          <th scope="col">Fact ids</th>
          <th scope="col">Source</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</details>

</main>

<footer>
  {{- /* THE SOURCE LIST IS DISCLOSED AND THE SENTENCE BELOW IT IS NOT. The
         list is two links per cited page and grows with the corpus; the
         sentence under it names the scope, the basis and the document on
         screen, and a reader must never have to open something to learn which
         fiscal year they are reading. */}}
  <details class="apparatus" id="sources-view">
    <summary>
      <h3>Sources for every year this page can show</h3>
      <span class="apparatus-hint">{{len .Sources}} document{{if ne (len .Sources) 1}}s{{end}}, every page this view cites</span>
    </summary>
    <ul>
      {{- range .Sources}}
      <li>
        {{if .PDFURL}}<a href="{{.PDFURL}}">{{.Title}}</a>{{else}}{{.Title}}{{end}}{{if .Publisher}}, {{.Publisher}}{{end}}
        {{- if .Pages}} —
        {{- range $i, $p := .Pages}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}
          <a href="{{$p.PDFURL}}">p{{$p.Number}} (PDF)</a> /
          <a href="{{$p.TextURL}}">extracted text</a>
        {{- end}}{{end}}
      </li>
      {{- end}}
    </ul>
    <p class="subtle">
      {{- /* THE FILE LIST FOLDS WITH THE SOURCE LIST because it is the same
             kind of thing: a set of links that grows with the corpus, useful
             to a reader who came for the data and furniture to one who did
             not. What stays outside is the sentence naming the scope, the
             basis and the file THIS page is drawn from. */}}
      Every data file the site publishes, each fetchable on its own:
      {{- range $i, $p := .Projections}}{{if $i}},{{end}} <a href="{{$p.Path}}">{{$p.Path}}</a>{{end}}.
    </p>
  </details>
  <p class="subtle">
    <!-- SCOPES, PLURAL, AND THAT IS THE ONE ENVELOPE DIFFERENCE THIS DOCUMENT
         CARRIES. Two schedules sit in one file because they are disjoint by
         kind, which projection-scopes-are-disjoint asserts; writing one of them
         here would publish half a document as the whole of it. -->
    Scope{{if gt (len .Scopes) 1}}s{{end}}
    {{- range $i, $s := .Scopes}}{{if $i}},{{end}} {{$s}}{{end}}, basis
    <span id="page-basis">{{.Basis}}</span>. Projection: {{.ProjectionBy}}. Packaged by {{.ExportedBy}}.
    <span data-year-path>This chart is drawn from <a href="{{.DataPath}}">{{.DataPath}}</a>.</span>
  </p>
</footer>

<script src="vendor/d3.min.js"></script>
<script src="vendor/d3-sankey.min.js"></script>
<script>window.FISC_CONFIG = {{.ConfigJSON}};</script>
<script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
